---
import '../../styles/global.css';
import MemoryCalculator from '../../components/artifacts/number-formats/MemoryCalculator.tsx';
import IntBits from '../../components/artifacts/number-formats/IntBits.tsx';
import FloatAnatomy from '../../components/artifacts/number-formats/FloatAnatomy.tsx';
import RangeVsPrecision, { FormatTable } from '../../components/artifacts/number-formats/RangeVsPrecision.tsx';
import Quiz from '../../components/artifacts/number-formats/Quiz.tsx';
import { Bite } from '../../components/artifacts/number-formats/ui.tsx';

import Sources from '../../components/Sources.astro';

const sources = [
  {
    title: 'A Visual Guide to Quantization',
    url: 'https://newsletter.maartengrootendorst.com/p/a-visual-guide-to-quantization',
    author: 'Maarten Grootendorst',
    note: 'The main reference for this series. This page is my own notes and examples built after reading it.',
  },
];

const title = 'Number formats: FP32, FP16, BF16 and INT8';
const description = 'A small, interactive guide to how computers store numbers, and why it matters for model size.';

const contents = [
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why do we care?' },
  { id: 'bits', label: 'Bits are switches' },
  { id: 'floats', label: 'Floats: sign, exponent, mantissa' },
  { id: 'range', label: 'Range vs precision' },
  { id: 'self-check', label: 'Self-check quiz' },
  { id: 'sources', label: 'Sources' },
];
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" />
    <link rel="canonical" href={new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site)} />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-white font-body text-neutral-900 antialiased">
    <main class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-5 pb-12 pt-10 sm:pt-16">
      <nav class="font-label text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em]">
        <a href="/" class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:no-underline">← Maula Irfani</a>
      </nav>

      <header class="mt-6 border-t-[6px] border-black pt-3">
        <p class="font-label text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]">Quantization · Part 1 · Study guide</p>
        <h1 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl font-bold leading-[1.1] tracking-tight">Number formats</h1>
        <p class="mt-4 font-body text-lg leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          Before we can shrink a model, we need to know how it stores its numbers. This guide is a set of small bites
          to learn that, with tiny examples you can play with. Click the bits. Break things.
        </p>
        <dl class="mt-5 grid grid-cols-[115px_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-sm">
          <dt class="font-display font-bold">Audience</dt>
          <dd>Anyone new to model quantization. No math background needed beyond powers of 2.</dd>
          <dt class="font-display font-bold">Scope</dt>
          <dd>How FP32, FP16, BF16 and INT8 store numbers, and the range and precision trade-off. Not yet: how to quantize a model.</dd>
        </dl>
      </header>

      <nav class="mt-10 border-t border-black pt-4" aria-labelledby="contents-title">
        <h2 id="contents-title" class="font-label text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]">Contents</h2>
        <ol class="mt-3 grid gap-x-8 text-sm sm:grid-cols-2">
          {
            contents.map((c, i) => (
              <li class="border-b border-neutral-300 py-1.5">
                <a href={`#${c.id}`} class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:no-underline">
                  <span class="font-label text-neutral-500">{i + 1}.</span> {c.label}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <section id="prerequisites" class="mt-10 border border-dashed border-neutral-600 px-4 py-3 text-sm">
        <h2 class="font-display text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]">Before you start</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          You should know what a power of 2 is: 2<sup>3</sup> = 2 × 2 × 2 = 8. That is all. Every other idea is
          explained on the page.
        </p>
      </section>

      <Bite
        id="why"
        n={1}
        title="Why do we care?"
        remember="Memory = number of parameters × bytes per number. Fewer bytes per number means a smaller model."
        question="But what is inside those bytes? Let us open one up."
      >
        <p>
          A model is a huge pile of numbers called <b>parameters</b>. Every number takes some bytes of memory. So:
        </p>
        <p class="border border-neutral-500 bg-white px-4 py-3 text-center font-label text-sm">
          memory = number of parameters × bytes per number
        </p>
        <p>
          Let us start small. Say each number takes <b>4 bytes</b> (this is the FP32 format, you will see why later).
          Now count up:
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[22rem] text-left text-sm tabular-nums">
            <thead class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-wide">
              <tr class="border-b border-black">
                <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-bold">Numbers</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-bold">× 4 bytes</th>
                <th class="py-2 font-bold">In plain words</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-neutral-400"><td class="py-2 pr-3">1</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">4 bytes</td><td class="py-2 text-neutral-600">tiny</td></tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-neutral-400"><td class="py-2 pr-3">1,000</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">4,000 bytes</td><td class="py-2 text-neutral-600">4 KB, a short text file</td></tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-neutral-400"><td class="py-2 pr-3">1 million</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">4 million bytes</td><td class="py-2 text-neutral-600">4 MB, one phone photo</td></tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-neutral-400"><td class="py-2 pr-3">1 billion</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">4 billion bytes</td><td class="py-2 text-neutral-600">4 GB, a small movie</td></tr>
              <tr class="font-bold"><td class="py-2 pr-3">70 billion</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">280 billion bytes</td><td class="py-2">280 GB</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Here is the shortcut. One billion bytes is one gigabyte (GB). So when the parameters are counted in
          billions, the answer is already in GB: <b>70 billion × 4 bytes = 280 GB</b>. Pick a model size below and
          watch what each format does.
        </p>
        <MemoryCalculator client:load />
        <p>Fewer bytes per number means a smaller model. So the goal is clear: use fewer bytes.</p>
      </Bite>

      <Bite
        id="bits"
        n={2}
        title="Bits are switches"
        remember="N bits give 2^N patterns. 8 bits give 256 patterns, so INT8 holds 256 whole numbers."
        question="INT8 only holds whole numbers like 0 to 255. How do we store 0.15625, or a huge number like one billion?"
      >
        <p>
          A <b>bit</b> is a switch that is either 0 or 1. Put 8 of them in a row and you have a <b>byte</b>. Each
          switch is worth a different amount: 1, 2, 4, 8, and so on. Add up the switches that are on.
        </p>
        <IntBits client:load />
        <p>
          With 8 bits there are 2<sup>8</sup> = 256 patterns. That is why INT8 can hold only 256 different values.
          And they are whole numbers only.
        </p>
      </Bite>

      <Bite
        id="floats"
        n={3}
        title="Floats: sign, exponent, mantissa"
        remember="A float is sign × (1 + mantissa) × 2^exponent. The exponent sets the scale. The mantissa sets the detail."
        question="FP16 and BF16 both use 16 bits, but they split them in different ways. Why have two, and what does each gain or lose?"
      >
        <p>
          The answer is <b>floating point</b>. It works like scientific notation. Take 0.15625 from the question above.
          We can write it as:
        </p>
        <p class="border border-neutral-500 bg-white px-4 py-3 text-center font-label text-sm">
          0.15625 = +1 × 1.25 × 2<sup>-3</sup>
        </p>
        <p>
          Three pieces: a <b>sign</b> (+ or -), an <b>exponent</b> (the power of 2, which sets the scale), and a{' '}
          <b>mantissa</b> (the digits after the point). The leading 1 in 1.25 is always there, so we do not store it.
          It is called the hidden 1.
        </p>
        <p>
          The exponent is stored with a <b>bias</b> so it can be negative. For FP32 the bias is 127. Click any bit
          below and watch the number change.
        </p>
        <FloatAnatomy client:load />
        <p>
          Now switch to FP16 and BF16 above. Same idea, but the bits are split in different ways. Look at how many
          bits the exponent and the mantissa get.
        </p>
      </Bite>

      <Bite
        id="range"
        n={4}
        title="Range vs precision"
        remember="More exponent bits mean more range. More mantissa bits mean more precision. With a fixed number of bits, you trade one for the other."
        question="Back to our memory problem: how far can we cut the bytes before the numbers break?"
      >
        <p>
          That split is the whole trade. More exponent bits give a wider <b>range</b>: how big and how small a number
          can be. More mantissa bits give more <b>precision</b>: how close neighbour numbers are. With a fixed number
          of bits, you trade one for the other.
        </p>
        <FormatTable />
        <p>
          FP16 and BF16 both use 16 bits, but they choose differently. FP16 keeps more precision. BF16 keeps the
          same range as FP32 and gives up precision. Type a number below and see what each format does to it. Try
          70000 and 0.00001.
        </p>
        <RangeVsPrecision client:load />
        <p>
          Notice that 0.1 is never exact in any format. Computers count in 2s, and 0.1 does not fit neatly. More
          mantissa bits just get closer.
        </p>
      </Bite>

      <Bite id="self-check" n={5} title="Self-check quiz">
        <p>
          Six questions to check what stuck from the four bites above. Pick an answer and the correct one is shown
          with the reason. Your score is not saved.
        </p>
        <Quiz client:load />
      </Bite>

      <p class="mt-12 border-t border-black pt-4 text-sm italic text-neutral-700">
        Next up, the answer to the last question: how to turn a big format into a small one (INT8, INT4) without
        losing too much.
      </p>

      <div id="sources" class="mt-8">
        <Sources items={sources} />
      </div>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
